# Hermetic build dependency bootstrap

Implement `tools/build/adapter-inbound/dependencies.py` with CPython 3.14.6 as
the declared
bootstrap and install public project dependencies into repository-owned pinned
locations without mutating global packages; validate proprietary or platform
toolchains as explicit external prerequisites instead of silently using
arbitrary host state.

## Acceptance criteria

- [x] Require CPython 3.14.6 for the supported workflow and emit a clear
  user-facing diagnostic for any other version.
- [x] Install or verify public dependencies deterministically under
  repository-owned `.dependencies/` and `.cache/` locations without global
  package mutation.
- [x] Treat Unreal Engine and unavoidable platform SDK or signing material as
  declared external prerequisites whose exact versions are checked rather
  than vendored.
- [x] Make repeated successful runs idempotent and persist machine-readable
  dependency evidence for later build steps.

## Dependencies

No explicit task dependency is required beyond the declared repository and
platform prerequisites.

## Evidence

`tools/build/adapter-inbound/dependencies.py` requires CPython 3.14.6, pins
Rustup 1.29.0 with
per-host SHA-256 digests, installs Rust/Cargo 1.97.1 below
`.dependencies/build/`, and uses `.cache/build/` for Cargo registry/build state.
It builds `validate-game` with `Cargo.lock` under `--locked`, publishes the
validator atomically, and writes `shar.build.dependencies.v1` evidence. Unreal,
platform SDKs, signing material, and Windows Visual Studio C++ tools remain
explicit external prerequisites instead of vendored state.

On Windows AMD64, the default no-toolchain-argument flow installed the
repo-local
MSVC Rust toolchain, initialized Visual Studio only for the child build process,
built the validator, and validated all 111 canonical game-manifest minimums. A
second run completed without reinstalling or changing the validator hash. The
explicit GNU 1.97.1 override path also remained functional, while Python 3.14.7
was rejected before success evidence could be published.
